WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
Red cells connect oxygen
delivery to a regulated production

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:03.633
response.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.829
Start with the circulating erythrocyte.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.423
Its reversible deformability permits
passage through narrow vessels.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.606
When oxygen availability falls,
the kidney releases erythropoietin.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.718
The marrow responds by increasing red cell production.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.540
Reticulocytes are newly released
cells, so they help us

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:22.407
follow that response.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.716
Production is only half of the cycle.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.877
Macrophages remove aging erythrocytes,
usually in the spleen.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.300
Globin is broken into amino acids, and the porphyrin

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.284
portion of heme becomes bilirubin.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:35.685
Iron is recycled.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.740
Transferrin transports iron through
plasma, while ferritin stores it

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:40.456
within cells.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.300
Keep these roles separate:
erythropoietin signals production, transferrin transports

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:47.970
iron, and ferritin stores iron.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:50.424
The figure provides visual orientation.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.127
These tested relationships come
from Chapter twenty eight, slides

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:57.457
twelve, thirty eight, thirty
nine, forty four, and forty

00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:57.660
five.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.307
The complete iron absorption
pathway remains a textbook gap.
